          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Is I10 a complete code, or does it need a fourth character?",
          "answer": "**Yes, I10 is a complete code.** It has three characters and no fourth character is needed. The coding summary \"Coding hypertension: I10 and the hypertensive disease family\" says I10 is \"unusual in being a complete code at three characters.\" Most ICD-10-CM categories need a fourth, fifth or sixth character before they are billable, but I10 takes none. Asking to \"add more specificity to I10\" is therefore not something the classification can do.\n\nThe general rule comes from \"ICD-10-CM code structure and the conventions that govern selection.\" Codes run three to seven characters. The three-character form is the category, and characters four and five add detail such as cause, site or severity. I10 sits in the hypertensive-disease block (I10 to I16) of Chapter 9, as described in \"ICD-10-CM Chapter 9: diseases of the circulatory system.\"\n\nThese sources are educational summaries, not the official ICD-10-CM tabular list or the official coding guidelines. If you need to apply this to a real claim or record, check the current official publication or ask a certified medical coder.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
